    question: "What do you understand by p-value?",
    slug: "what-do-you-understand-by-p-value",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED] Fresher / Entry-Level interview question: What do you understand by p-value?",
    sampleAnswer: "A p-value is the probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of observing a test statistic at least as extreme as the one obtained from the sample. It measures how incompatible the observed data are with the null hypothesis.\n\nA small p-value means the observed result would be relatively unusual if the null hypothesis were true, so we have evidence against the null hypothesis. It does not mean that the probability that the null hypothesis is true is small.\n\nThe p-value must be interpreted together with the significance level, effect size, confidence interval, study design, and practical importance. A statistically significant result can still have a tiny business impact, while a practically important effect may fail to reach significance with a small sample.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nLevel: Fresher / Entry-Level\n\nAnswer:\nA p-value is the probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of observing a test statistic at least as extreme as the one obtained from the sample. It measures how incompatible the observed data are with the null hypothesis.\n\nA small p-value means the observed result would be relatively unusual if the null hypothesis were true, so we have evidence against the null hypothesis. It does not mean that the probability that the null hypothesis is true is small.\n\nThe p-value must be interpreted together with the significance level, effect size, confidence interval, study design, and practical importance. A statistically significant result can still have a tiny business impact, while a practically important effect may fail to reach significance with a small sample.\n\nExample:\nSuppose an A/B test compares two conversion rates and produces p = 0.02. With alpha = 0.05, the result is statistically significant under the test assumptions, so we reject the null hypothesis. We still need to examine the effect size and confidence interval to decide whether the change is practically valuable.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the direct answer, show the reasoning, then give a small example. For coding/SQL questions, state complexity and important edge cases. For case studies, clarify the business objective before choosing a model.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can apply data-science concepts correctly, reason about assumptions and trade-offs, and turn a business or coding problem into a reliable solution.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nA p-value is evidence against H0 under H0; it is not the probability that H0 is true.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy? What are the assumptions? What are the edge cases? How would you test or validate it? What alternative approach would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a memorized definition without reasoning; ignoring assumptions; using the wrong metric; overlooking NULLs/duplicates in SQL; ignoring leakage or validation in ML; or failing to state complexity for coding questions.\n\nSource Evidence:\nReported in: Meesho Data Scientist-1 interview, Bangalore, April 2025.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------",
