    question: "How would you implement mean, median and mode in core Python without using library functions?",
    slug: "how-would-you-implement-mean-median-and-mode-in-core-python-without-using-library-functions",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED] Intermediate interview question: How would you implement mean, median and mode in core Python without using library functions?",
    sampleAnswer: "Mean is the arithmetic average, median is the middle value after sorting, and mode is the most frequent value.\n\nFor an unsorted list, a mean requires one pass and a running sum. A median can be obtained by sorting, typically O(n log n), then selecting the middle element(s). Mode can be computed with a frequency dictionary in expected O(n) time.\n\nMedian is generally more robust to extreme outliers than mean, while mode is especially useful for categorical or discrete data.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nMean is the arithmetic average, median is the middle value after sorting, and mode is the most frequent value.\n\nFor an unsorted list, a mean requires one pass and a running sum. A median can be obtained by sorting, typically O(n log n), then selecting the middle element(s). Mode can be computed with a frequency dictionary in expected O(n) time.\n\nMedian is generally more robust to extreme outliers than mean, while mode is especially useful for categorical or discrete data.\n\nExample:\ndef stats(values):\n        if not values:\n            raise ValueError(\"empty input\")\n        mean = sum(values) / len(values)\n        ordered = sorted(values)\n        n = len(ordered)\n        median = ordered[n // 2] if n % 2 else (ordered[n//2 - 1] + ordered[n//2]) / 2\n\n        counts = {}\n        for x in values:\n            counts[x] = counts.get(x, 0) + 1\n        mode = max(counts, key=counts.get)\n\n        return mean, median, mode\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the direct answer, show the reasoning, then give a small example. For coding/SQL questions, state complexity and important edge cases. For case studies, clarify the business objective before choosing a model.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can apply data-science concepts correctly, reason about assumptions and trade-offs, and turn a business or coding problem into a reliable solution.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nDefinition, assumptions, practical application, trade-offs, edge cases, and validation.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy? What are the assumptions? What are the edge cases? How would you test or validate it? What alternative approach would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a memorized definition without reasoning; ignoring assumptions; using the wrong metric; overlooking NULLs/duplicates in SQL; ignoring leakage or validation in ML; or failing to state complexity for coding questions.\n\nSource Evidence:\nReported in: Nykaa Data Scientist 2 interview, May 2026.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------",
