    question: "Write SQL to find the third-highest salary.",
    slug: "write-sql-to-find-the-third-highest-salary",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED] Intermediate interview question: Write SQL to find the third-highest salary.",
    sampleAnswer: "There are multiple correct SQL interpretations depending on whether “third-highest” means the third distinct salary or the third row after sorting.\n\nFor the third distinct salary, a window-function solution is explicit:\n\n    SELECT salary\n    FROM (\n        SELECT salary,\n               DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n        FROM employees\n    ) x\n    WHERE rnk = 3;\n\nDENSE_RANK() handles duplicate salaries correctly when distinct salary levels are required.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nThere are multiple correct SQL interpretations depending on whether “third-highest” means the third distinct salary or the third row after sorting.\n\nFor the third distinct salary, a window-function solution is explicit:\n\n    SELECT salary\n    FROM (\n        SELECT salary,\n               DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n        FROM employees\n    ) x\n    WHERE rnk = 3;\n\nDENSE_RANK() handles duplicate salaries correctly when distinct salary levels are required.\n\nExample:\nSELECT salary\n    FROM (\n        SELECT salary,\n               DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n        FROM employees\n    ) ranked\n    WHERE rnk = 3;\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the direct answer, show the reasoning, then give a small example. For coding/SQL questions, state complexity and important edge cases. For case studies, clarify the business objective before choosing a model.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can apply data-science concepts correctly, reason about assumptions and trade-offs, and turn a business or coding problem into a reliable solution.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nAlways define row grain, join keys, aggregation level, NULL behavior, and duplicate risk.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy? What are the assumptions? What are the edge cases? How would you test or validate it? What alternative approach would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a memorized definition without reasoning; ignoring assumptions; using the wrong metric; overlooking NULLs/duplicates in SQL; ignoring leakage or validation in ML; or failing to state complexity for coding questions.\n\nSource Evidence:\nReported in: Nielsen Data Scientist interview.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------",
