    question: "Write a Pandas operation to separate first and last names from a full-name column, remove duplicate columns and handle missing values.",
    slug: "write-a-pandas-operation-to-separate-first-and-last-names-from-a-full-name-column-remove-duplicate-columns-and-handle-missing-values",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED] Intermediate interview question: Write a Pandas operation to separate first and last names from a full-name column, remove duplicate columns and handle missing values.",
    sampleAnswer: "Pandas provides vectorized string and missing-value operations for this task.\n\n    parts = df[\"full_name\"].str.split(n=1, expand=True)\n    df[\"first_name\"] = parts[0]\n    df[\"last_name\"] = parts[1]\n    df = df.drop(columns=[\"unwanted_column\"])\n    df[\"age\"] = df[\"age\"].fillna(df[\"age\"].median())\n    df = df.drop_duplicates()\n\nThe exact missing-value strategy depends on the meaning and distribution of the column. For names, splitting rules should also account for single-name records and unusual formats.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nPandas provides vectorized string and missing-value operations for this task.\n\n    parts = df[\"full_name\"].str.split(n=1, expand=True)\n    df[\"first_name\"] = parts[0]\n    df[\"last_name\"] = parts[1]\n    df = df.drop(columns=[\"unwanted_column\"])\n    df[\"age\"] = df[\"age\"].fillna(df[\"age\"].median())\n    df = df.drop_duplicates()\n\nThe exact missing-value strategy depends on the meaning and distribution of the column. For names, splitting rules should also account for single-name records and unusual formats.\n\nExample:\nA DataFrame with full_name = [\"Ada Lovelace\", \"Alan Turing\", None] can be split with str.split(..., expand=True) and then missing values handled according to the data contract.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the direct answer, show the reasoning, then give a small example. For coding/SQL questions, state complexity and important edge cases. For case studies, clarify the business objective before choosing a model.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can apply data-science concepts correctly, reason about assumptions and trade-offs, and turn a business or coding problem into a reliable solution.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nDefinition, assumptions, practical application, trade-offs, edge cases, and validation.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy? What are the assumptions? What are the edge cases? How would you test or validate it? What alternative approach would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a memorized definition without reasoning; ignoring assumptions; using the wrong metric; overlooking NULLs/duplicates in SQL; ignoring leakage or validation in ML; or failing to state complexity for coding questions.\n\nSource Evidence:\nReported in: Nielsen Data Scientist interview.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------",
