    question: "Explain precision and recall.",
    slug: "explain-precision-and-recall",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED TOPIC] Intermediate interview question: Explain precision and recall.",
    sampleAnswer: "Precision answers: among the items predicted positive, how many were actually positive?\n\nPrecision = TP / (TP + FP)\n\nRecall answers: among all actual positive items, how many did the model find?\n\nRecall = TP / (TP + FN)\n\nUse precision when false positives are particularly costly, and recall when false negatives are particularly costly. In many applications, the threshold should be selected according to business costs rather than automatically choosing 0.5.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED TOPIC]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nPrecision answers: among the items predicted positive, how many were actually positive?\n\nPrecision = TP / (TP + FP)\n\nRecall answers: among all actual positive items, how many did the model find?\n\nRecall = TP / (TP + FN)\n\nUse precision when false positives are particularly costly, and recall when false negatives are particularly costly. In many applications, the threshold should be selected according to business costs rather than automatically choosing 0.5.\n\nExample:\nIf a fraud model flags 100 transactions and 80 are actually fraud, precision is 80%. If there are 200 fraudulent transactions in total, recall is 40%.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the direct answer, show the reasoning, then give a small example. For coding/SQL questions, state complexity and important edge cases. For case studies, clarify the business objective before choosing a model.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can apply data-science concepts correctly, reason about assumptions and trade-offs, and turn a business or coding problem into a reliable solution.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nPrecision focuses on false positives; recall focuses on false negatives.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy? What are the assumptions? What are the edge cases? How would you test or validate it? What alternative approach would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a memorized definition without reasoning; ignoring assumptions; using the wrong metric; overlooking NULLs/duplicates in SQL; ignoring leakage or validation in ML; or failing to state complexity for coding questions.\n\nSource Evidence:\nReported in: Nielsen Data Scientist interview.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------",
