    question: "Reverse a linked list.",
    slug: "reverse-a-linked-list",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED] Intermediate interview question: Reverse a linked list.",
    sampleAnswer: "An iterative linked-list reversal changes each node’s next pointer to point to the previous node. Maintain three references: previous, current, and next.\n\n    def reverse(head):\n        prev = None\n        cur = head\n\n        while cur:\n            nxt = cur.next\n            cur.next = prev\n            prev = cur\n            cur = nxt\n\n        return prev\n\nTime complexity is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1). The critical edge cases are an empty list and a single-node list.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nAn iterative linked-list reversal changes each node’s next pointer to point to the previous node. Maintain three references: previous, current, and next.\n\n    def reverse(head):\n        prev = None\n        cur = head\n\n        while cur:\n            nxt = cur.next\n            cur.next = prev\n            prev = cur\n            cur = nxt\n\n        return prev\n\nTime complexity is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1). The critical edge cases are an empty list and a single-node list.\n\nExample:\nFor 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> None, reversing produces 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> None.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the direct answer, show the reasoning, then give a small example. For coding/SQL questions, state complexity and important edge cases. For case studies, clarify the business objective before choosing a model.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can apply data-science concepts correctly, reason about assumptions and trade-offs, and turn a business or coding problem into a reliable solution.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nDefinition, assumptions, practical application, trade-offs, edge cases, and validation.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy? What are the assumptions? What are the edge cases? How would you test or validate it? What alternative approach would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a memorized definition without reasoning; ignoring assumptions; using the wrong metric; overlooking NULLs/duplicates in SQL; ignoring leakage or validation in ML; or failing to state complexity for coding questions.\n\nSource Evidence:\nReported in: BusinessNEXT Data Scientist interview experience.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------",
