    question: "Write SQL involving joins and a subquery.",
    slug: "write-sql-involving-joins-and-a-subquery",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED] Intermediate interview question: Write SQL involving joins and a subquery.",
    sampleAnswer: "A JOIN combines rows across tables using a relationship, while a subquery computes an intermediate result that can be used for filtering or comparison.\n\n    SELECT e.name, e.salary\n    FROM employees e\n    JOIN departments d\n      ON d.id = e.department_id\n    WHERE e.salary > (\n        SELECT AVG(salary)\n        FROM employees\n    );\n\nA strong answer explains join cardinality, NULL behavior, duplicate rows, and whether a window function or CTE would make the logic clearer.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nA JOIN combines rows across tables using a relationship, while a subquery computes an intermediate result that can be used for filtering or comparison.\n\n    SELECT e.name, e.salary\n    FROM employees e\n    JOIN departments d\n      ON d.id = e.department_id\n    WHERE e.salary > (\n        SELECT AVG(salary)\n        FROM employees\n    );\n\nA strong answer explains join cardinality, NULL behavior, duplicate rows, and whether a window function or CTE would make the logic clearer.\n\nExample:\nExample: To find departments with more than five employees, group by department and use HAVING COUNT(*) > 5 rather than putting the aggregate condition in WHERE.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the direct answer, show the reasoning, then give a small example. For coding/SQL questions, state complexity and important edge cases. For case studies, clarify the business objective before choosing a model.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can apply data-science concepts correctly, reason about assumptions and trade-offs, and turn a business or coding problem into a reliable solution.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nAlways define row grain, join keys, aggregation level, NULL behavior, and duplicate risk.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy? What are the assumptions? What are the edge cases? How would you test or validate it? What alternative approach would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a memorized definition without reasoning; ignoring assumptions; using the wrong metric; overlooking NULLs/duplicates in SQL; ignoring leakage or validation in ML; or failing to state complexity for coding questions.\n\nSource Evidence:\nReported in: BusinessNEXT Data Scientist interview experience.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------",
