    question: "Find the longest increasing subsequence.",
    slug: "find-the-longest-increasing-subsequence",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED] Intermediate interview question: Find the longest increasing subsequence.",
    sampleAnswer: "The Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) asks for the longest subsequence whose values are strictly increasing; the selected elements do not need to be adjacent.\n\nA straightforward dynamic-programming solution defines dp[i] as the length of the longest increasing subsequence ending at index i, giving O(n²) time and O(n) space.\n\nA more advanced solution maintains a tails array and uses binary search, achieving O(n log n) time. The latter is usually the stronger interview solution when only the length is required.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nThe Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) asks for the longest subsequence whose values are strictly increasing; the selected elements do not need to be adjacent.\n\nA straightforward dynamic-programming solution defines dp[i] as the length of the longest increasing subsequence ending at index i, giving O(n²) time and O(n) space.\n\nA more advanced solution maintains a tails array and uses binary search, achieving O(n log n) time. The latter is usually the stronger interview solution when only the length is required.\n\nExample:\nFor [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18], one LIS is [2, 3, 7, 101], so the length is 4.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the direct answer, show the reasoning, then give a small example. For coding/SQL questions, state complexity and important edge cases. For case studies, clarify the business objective before choosing a model.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can apply data-science concepts correctly, reason about assumptions and trade-offs, and turn a business or coding problem into a reliable solution.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nDefinition, assumptions, practical application, trade-offs, edge cases, and validation.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy? What are the assumptions? What are the edge cases? How would you test or validate it? What alternative approach would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nGiving a memorized definition without reasoning; ignoring assumptions; using the wrong metric; overlooking NULLs/duplicates in SQL; ignoring leakage or validation in ML; or failing to state complexity for coding questions.\n\nSource Evidence:\nReported in: Axtria Data Scientist interview process.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------",
